Git Commands: Uses and Scenarios
Git Configuration
•	Command: git config --global user.name "Your Name"
Use: Sets your Git username globally
Scenario: Run once after installing Git

•	Command: git config --global user.email "you@example.com"
Use: Sets your Git email globally
Scenario: Required for commit history

•	Command: git config --list
Use: Lists all Git configuration settings
Scenario: To verify your Git setup

Repository Setup
•	Command: git init
Use: Initializes a new Git repository
Scenario: When starting a new project

•	Command: git clone <url>
Use: Clones an existing repository
Scenario: When working on an existing project

Staging and Committing
•	Command: git status
Use: Shows the status of changes
Scenario: To see what’s staged, unstaged, or untracked

•	Command: git add <file>
Use: Stages a specific file
Scenario: When you want to commit only certain files

•	Command: git add .
Use: Stages all changes in the directory
Scenario: When you're ready to commit everything

•	Command: git commit -m "message"
Use: Commits staged changes with a message
Scenario: To save a snapshot of your changes

Branching and Merging
•	Command: git branch
Use: Lists all branches
Scenario: To view or manage branches

•	Command: git branch <branch-name>
Use: Creates a new branch
Scenario: To work on a new feature or fix

•	Command: git checkout <branch-name>
Use: Switches to a branch
Scenario: To move between branches

•	Command: git switch <branch-name>
Use: Alternative to checkout for switching
Scenario: Easier syntax for switching branches

•	Command: git merge <branch-name>
Use: Merges a branch into the current one
Scenario: To combine changes from another branch

Remote Repositories
•	Command: git remote -v
Use: Shows remote URLs
Scenario: To verify remote connections

•	Command: git fetch
Use: Downloads changes but doesn’t merge
Scenario: To see what’s new before merging

•	Command: git pull
Use: Fetches and merges changes
Scenario: To update your local branch

•	Command: git push
Use: Uploads local commits to remote
Scenario: To share your changes

Logs and Diffs
•	Command: git log
Use: Shows commit history
Scenario: To review past commits

•	Command: git log --oneline
Use: Condensed commit history
Scenario: For a quick overview

•	Command: git diff
Use: Shows file differences
Scenario: To see what changed before committing

•	Command: git show <commit>
Use: Shows details of a specific commit
Scenario: To inspect a particular change

Stash and Revert
•	Command: git stash
Use: Saves uncommitted changes temporarily
Scenario: When switching branches without committing

•	Command: git stash pop
Use: Restores stashed changes
Scenario: To continue working on stashed changes

•	Command: git revert <commit>
Use: Reverts a specific commit
Scenario: To undo a commit without rewriting history

•	Command: git reset <file>
Use: Unstages a file
Scenario: To remove a file from the staging area

Advanced Commands
•	Command: git reflog
Use: Shows history of HEAD changes
Scenario: To recover lost commits

•	Command: git cherry-pick <hash>
Use: Applies a specific commit from another branch
Scenario: To copy a commit without merging the whole branch

•	Command: git tag <name>
Use: Tags a commit
Scenario: To mark versions or releases

•	Command: git bisect
Use: Finds the commit that introduced a bug
Scenario: For debugging regressions

Other Useful Commands
•	Command: git rm <file>
Use: Removes a file from the repo and stages the deletion
Scenario: To delete a file from version control

•	Command: git mv <old> <new>
Use: Renames a file and stages the change
Scenario: To rename files in Git

•	Command: git clean -fd
Use: Removes untracked files and directories
Scenario: To clean up working directory

•	Command: git archive
Use: Creates a zip/tar archive of the repository
Scenario: To export a snapshot of the project

•	Command: git blame <file>
Use: Shows who last modified each line of a file
Scenario: To trace changes line-by-line






